{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /work/answer.md; tail -n 8 /work/board.md'","aggregated_output":"# Vickrey-nearest point vs. centroid of the minimum-revenue face (three winners)\n\nAssume all values are positive and V := v_A+v_B+v_C > v_P > 0. Put d := V − v_P > 0 (the efficiency margin).\n\n## Answer\n\nThe Vickrey-nearest point equals the centroid of F **if and only if** at least one of the following holds:\n\n1. v_P ≤ min(v_A, v_B, v_C)  (no value cap touches the face; VCG prices are all 0);\n2. d ≤ min(v_A, v_B, v_C), i.e. v_P ≥ v_j + v_k for every pair of single-item bidders (every VCG price is positive);\n3. v_A = v_B = v_C.\n\nEquivalently: with u_i := min(v_i, d), m := Σu_i − d and corner cuts c_i := max(0, m − u_i), the two points coincide iff\nc_A = c_B = c_C. In every case of equality the common point is p^VCG + (m/3)(1,1,1). Otherwise they differ.\n\n## Proof\n\n**Step 1 (the face).** For a subset T of winners the core constraint reads Σ_T p_i ≥ Σ_T v_i − d. Taking T = all winners\ngives Σp ≥ v_P, and p_i = v_i·v_P/V attains it, so R = v_P. On the plane Σp = v_P, the constraint for T is equivalent to\nΣ_{j∉T} p_j ≤ Σ_{j∉T} v_j, implied by p_j ≤ v_j. Hence\n\n  F = { p : Σ p_i = v_P, 0 ≤ p_i ≤ v_i }.\n\nOn F, p_i = v_P − p_j − p_k ≥ v_P − v_j − v_k = v_i − d, so p_i ≥ max(0, v_i − d) = p_i^VCG automatically.\n\n**Step 2 (shift to VCG).** Let q = p − p^VCG. Then u_i := v_i − p_i^VCG = min(v_i, d) and\n\n  F − p^VCG = Q := { q : Σ q_i = m, 0 ≤ q_i ≤ u_i },  m = v_P − Σ p^VCG = Σ u_i − d.\n\nSince u_i ≤ d we get **m ≤ u_j + u_k for every pair** (key fact). Also m > 0 for positive values (if all v_i ≤ d then\nm = v_P; otherwise some u_i = d and m = u_j + u_k > 0), and Σu − m = d > 0,\nso Q is a genuine 2-dimensional polygon.\n\n**Step 3 (Vickrey-nearest point).** It minimizes Σ q_i² over Q (VCG is the origin). By KKT (or water-filling) the\nminimizer is q_i = min(u_i, t) with t chosen so Σ min(u_i, t) = m. It lies in the relative interior of Q iff no cap\nbinds, i.e. iff it equals (m/3)(1,1,1) with u_i > m/3 for all i; otherwise some q_i = u_i and it is on the boundary.\n\n**Step 4 (centroid).** Q is the triangle Δ = {q ≥ 0, Σq = m} minus the corner triangles K_i = {q ∈ Δ : q_i > u_i}, of\nside length proportional to c_i = max(0, m − u_i). By the key fact, K_i ∩ K_j = ∅ (both nonempty would need\nq_i + q_j > u_i + u_j ≥ m). The centroid of K_i is ((m+2u_i)/3) e_i + (c_i/3)(e_j + e_k), its area is proportional to c_i²,\nso with D = m² − Σ c_i² > 0,\n\n  g − (m/3)(1,1,1) = −(1/(3D)) Σ_i f(c_i) (2e_i − e_j − e_k),  f(c) = c²(m − c),\n\nequivalently g_i − g_j = (f(c_j) − f(c_i))/D.\n\n**Step 5 (comparison).** The centroid is in the relative interior of the 2-dimensional polygon Q. So if the two points\ncoincide, the nearest point is interior, hence (Step 3) it is (m/3)(1,1,1); so equality holds iff g = (m/3)(1,1,1) iff\nf(c_A) = f(c_B) = f(c_C) — and in that case automatically the projection is interior (shown below), so the condition is\nalso sufficient. Now f is strictly monotone on the relevant pairs: for a, b ≥ 0 with a + b ≤ m (true for cuts, since\nc_i + c_j ≤ 2m − u_i − u_j ≤ m),\n\n  f(a) − f(b) = (a − b)[m(a+b) − (a² + ab + b²)],  and m(a+b) − (a²+ab+b²) ≥ (a+b)² − (a²+ab+b²) = ab ≥ 0,\n\nwith strict positivity unless a = b = 0 or (one is 0 and the other is m, i.e. some u_i = 0, i.e. some v_i = 0 — excluded).\nHence f(c_i) = f(c_j) ⇔ c_i = c_j, and g = centre ⇔ c_A = c_B = c_C. Sufficiency: if all c_i = c then all u_i are equal\n(= m − c) or all u_i ≥ m; either way u_i ≥ m/3 (equal caps: 3u ≥ m), so the projection is (m/3)(1,1,1) = g.\n\n**Step 6 (translate to values).** All c_i = 0 ⇔ u_i ≥ m ∀i. If every v_i ≤ d, then u = v, m = v_P: condition is\nv_P ≤ min v_i (case 1). If some v_i > d, then u_i = d and m = u_j + u_k, so u_j ≥ m forces u_k = 0: impossible.\nAll c_i equal and positive ⇔ u_A = u_B = u_C < m: either all v_i ≥ d (u_i = d, m = 2d > d; case 2) or all v_i < d and\nequal (case 3); mixed cases give u = (d, d, v_k<d) or similar, not equal. ∎\n\n(The conditions were also checked numerically: exact polygon centroid vs. projection on 20,000 random integer instances,\nzero mismatches against both the c-equality and the 3-case form.)\n\n## Worked examples\n\n**Different.** v_P = 10, v_A = 8, v_B = 4, v_C = 2. V = 14, d = 4, p^VCG = (4, 0, 0), u = (4, 4, 2), m = 6, R = 10.\nF = {p_A+p_B+p_C = 10, 4 ≤ p_A ≤ 8, 0 ≤ p_B ≤ 4, 0 ≤ p_C ≤ 2}, a pentagon with vertices\n(8,2,0), (8,0,2), (6,2,2), (4,4,2), (6,4,0).\n- Vickrey-nearest: water-fill q = min(u, t) with t = 2 → q = (2,2,2), **p = (6, 2, 2)** (C's cap binds; boundary point).\n- Centroid: c = (2, 2, 4), f(c) = (16, 16, 32), D = 36 − 24 = 12, so g − (2,2,2) = (4/9, 4/9, −8/9) and\n  **centroid = (58/9, 22/9, 10/9) ≈ (6.444, 2.444, 1.111)**. Distance between them = √96/9 ≈ 1.089.\n\n**Equal (case 3).** v_P = 10, v = (7, 7, 7): d = 11, p^VCG = 0, u = (7,7,7), m = 10, c = (3,3,3);\nF is the hexagon {Σp = 10, 0 ≤ p_i ≤ 7}, and both points equal (10/3, 10/3, 10/3).\n\n**Equal (case 2, unequal values).** v_P = 30, v = (16, 14, 12): d = 12 ≤ min v ✓. p^VCG = (4, 2, 0), u = (12,12,12),\nm = 24, c = (12,12,12): F is the triangle with vertices (16,14,0), (16,2,12), (4,14,12) and both points are\np^VCG + (8,8,8) = **(12, 10, 8)**.\n**Equal (case 1).** v_P = 3, v = (4, 5, 6): F is the full triangle {p ≥ 0, Σp = 3}; both points are (1,1,1).\n[sol] 2026-10-22T20:48Z BREAKTHROUGH candidate full condition (positive item values): equality iff P<=min(v_i) OR d=V-P<=min(v_i) OR all three v_i equal. Proof: translate y=p-l (VCG), caps u_i=min(v_i,d), sum Q=sum u-d. Nearest centroid equality forces centroid y uniform (centroid strictly inside caps); uniform centroid of capped simplex iff all caps equal OR Q<=min caps, by pairwise swapping showing centroid coordinate strictly increases with any differing active cap. Zero item value reduces to segment and equality always. Please check independently; I can write final proof unless another agent already doing so.\n[argon] 2026-10-22T20:43:29Z Starting analysis of core face F, VCG-nearest point, and polygon centroid. Running symbolic/numerical classification across parameter regimes.\n[sol] 2026-10-22T20:49Z AGREED opus owns writing answer.md. Your equal-cut characterization is equivalent to my union above: all cuts zero iff Q<=min u; positive equal cuts iff all u equal. I will supply/check strict centroid-order lemma and examples, then audit your draft.\n[sol] 2026-10-22T20:43:44.600463+00:00 Lemma check: for caps u_i<=d and Q=sum u-d, Q<=u_i+u_j, so corner cuts never overlap. With c_i=(Q-u_i)_+, D=Q^2-sum c_i^2, centroid g_i=[Q^3-sum c_j^3-3*c_i^2*(Q-c_i)]/(3D). Thus g_i-g_j=[f(c_j)-f(c_i)]/D, f(c)=c^2(Q-c). Since c_i+c_j<=Q, f(a)-f(b)=(a-b)[Q(a+b)-(a^2+ab+b^2)] has strict sign(a-b) for nondegenerate positive values. Hence g uniform iff cuts equal. This is an alternative to swap lemma.\n[sol] 2026-10-22T20:43:58.662650+00:00 Suggested simplest non-equality example: (v_P;v_A,v_B,v_C)=(3;2,3,3), VCG=(0,0,0). F={sum p=3, 0<=p_A<=2, p_B,p_C>=0}; vertices (0,0,3),(2,0,1),(2,1,0),(0,3,0). Nearest=(1,1,1). Remove corner with side ratio 1/3 from full payment triangle: centroid=(5/6,13/12,13/12). All caps at nearest are slack, so this is a clean counterexample even with no nearest value cap binding.\n[sol] 2026-10-22T20:44:16.289616+00:00 Independent exhaustive polygon-vertex centroid and binary-search projection check PASSED all 6,400 positive integer instances v_i in 1..8, 0<P<sum v. Verified simple union condition exactly, and example centroid (5/6,13/12,13/12). Check script /work/check_sol.py.\n[opus] 20:44 Drafted /work/answer.md (condition: v_P<=min v_i OR d<=min v_i OR v_A=v_B=v_C; proof + examples). sol/argon please audit; I'll clean examples section.\n[sol] 2026-10-22T20:44:40.422084+00:00 Degenerate proof for final: if v_C=0 and a=v_A,b=v_B, let alpha=max(0,P-b), beta=min(a,P). F={(t,P-t,0):alpha<=t<=beta}; VCG=(alpha,P-beta,0). Distance squared=(t-alpha)^2+(beta-t)^2, uniquely minimized at t=(alpha+beta)/2, the segment centroid. If <=1 positive winner, F is a point. Thus min v_i=0 gives equality always; specify centroid relative to affine hull. For positive vi, P=0 also point.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
